WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.010
- Good afternoon, ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:14.990
My name is Jeff Brenzel
and I teach here at Yale

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:18.790
in the Philosophy and
Humanities departments.

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:22.870
In my role as Master of
Timothy Dwight College

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:26.690
I am also the official steward
of the Chubb Fellowship

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.700
at Yale University.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.710
So it is on behalf of
the Chubb Fellowship,

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:35.540
of Timothy Dwight College,
of the President and officers

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:38.810
and fellows of Yale
University, the faculty

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:41.250
and staff and students of Yale,

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.570
and the entire New Haven area community

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:47.310
that we welcome His
Holiness the 17th Karmapa

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:51.943
and His delegation to our
community and to our campus.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.890
I want to recognize
briefly the co-sponsors

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:59.290
and the other collaborators
who have worked hard to arrange

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:02.050
and support the visit of His Holiness

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.623
and His delegation here to Yale.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.910
In addition to the Chubb Fellowship,

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:12.380
these are the Yale Himalaya Initiative,

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.410
the Forum on Religion and Ecology,

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:18.020
the Yale Religious Studies Department,

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:21.780
the Yale School of Forestry
and Environmental Studies,

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.270
the Yale Divinity School
and the MacMillan Center

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.933
for International and Area Studies.

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.970
We are particularly grateful, of course,

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:36.420
to His Holiness himself
for His generosity of time

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.740
and spirit in making himself
astonishingly available

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.960
during His time here, putting
forth a strenuous effort

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.960
to meet or speak or connect
with so many communities within

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.340
and without the university who hoped

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.350
for the opportunity to see and hear Him

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:58.773
during His stay here at Yale.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:04.160
I want to make special
mention of the many members

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:07.660
of the Tibetan community from
the entire eastern region

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.510
of the United States,
who have traveled here

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:13.630
to be with us this afternoon, as well

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:16.070
as many other Buddhist
brothers and sisters

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:18.480
from other lands and places.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.926
To all of you I want to
extend our warmest welcome.

00:02:22.926 --> 00:02:26.093
(audience applauding)

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.920
Here this afternoon His Holiness

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:41.500
will be presenting this year's
Chubb Fellowship address.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:43.430
And I should say just a brief word

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:45.543
about the fellowship itself.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:50.000
Hendon Chubb was an 1895 graduate

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.090
of Yale's Sheffield Scientific School,

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:58.010
and in 1936 he made a substantial
gift to the university

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:01.830
with the intent of
encouraging Yale's students

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:06.490
to take a particular
interest in public service.

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:11.040
Since 1949, as Hendon
Chubb himself approved,

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.130
the university has used his endowment

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:17.230
to underwrite a visiting speakers program

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:21.690
bringing extraordinary
public figures to the campus.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:24.670
Past Chubb fellows include four presidents

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:28.690
of the United States, presidents
of eight other countries,

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:31.930
Nobel and other
international prize winners,

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:35.940
and more than 150 other regional, national

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.060
and internationally prominent persons,

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.050
all of whom have been noteworthy
in some particular way

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:46.360
for their personal distinction in service

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.630
to the common good of all.

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:54.630
It is now my honor to ask
Andrew Quintman to the podium.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.910
Professor Quintman teaches at Yale

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:00.420
in the Department of Religious Studies,

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.440
where he specializes in the
Buddhist traditions of Tibet

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.270
and the Himalaya.

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:09.770
His work surveys Buddhist
literature and history,

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:12.640
sacred geography and pilgrimage,

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.060
and the visual cultures of
the wider Himalayan region.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:20.630
He has written several books
on the early Tibetan saint

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:24.210
Milarepa, including an English translation

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:28.140
of The Life of Milarepa,
who is a founding figure

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:31.513
in the Karmapa's Lineage.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:34.957
Please welcome professor Quintman.

00:04:34.957 --> 00:04:38.124
(audience applauding)

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:47.710
- Thank you, Jeff.

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:48.603
Good afternoon.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:52.370
It is my great pleasure and distinct honor

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:55.600
to introduce His Holiness the 17th Karmapa

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.010
Ogyen Trinley Dorje, who
will share with us today

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:02.870
His insight and vision
on three crucial topics

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:07.833
for our time: compassion,
religion, and the environment.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:14.670
In 1949, at the request of
Yale's Beinecke Rare Book

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:17.990
and Manuscript Library, 100 volumes

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:20.370
of the Tibetan Buddhist Canon were printed

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:24.970
from wood blocks in Lhasa,
wrapped in leather satchels

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:27.470
and loaded onto ponies.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:30.120
They traveled by caravan to Calcutta

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:34.410
and then freighter to New Haven,
where they arrived in 1950

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:38.370
and were installed in the
library with great ceremony.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:42.830
This gift presented to Yale
by the current Dalai Lama,

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:45.400
who was only 14 years old
at the time and still living

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:50.280
in Lhasa, planted a seed of
Yale's connection to Tibet,

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:54.300
a seed that has now
taken root and blossomed

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.723
with the visit by His
Holiness the Karmapa.

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:00.700
The Karmapa is part of a Tibetan tradition

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:05.060
of formally recognized
reincarnations known as Tulku,

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:08.400
wherein a young child is
identified as the re-embodiment

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.460
of a deceased master.

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:13.500
The Karmapas are the head
of the Karma Kagyu branch

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:15.640
of Tibetan Buddhism, and are the oldest

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:18.630
such reincarnation lineage
in the Himalayan region,

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:21.760
extending back some 900 years.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.653
His Holiness Ogyen Trinley
Dorje is the 17th in his line.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:31.070
The first Karmapa, Dusum Khyenpa, was born

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:34.410
in the early 12th century, and
is said to have left a letter

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:37.870
for telling the conditions
of his future rebirth.

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:41.290
Since then such prediction
letters have become a hallmark

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:44.730
of the lineage, and together
with other religious signs,

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:47.793
have been used to identify
subsequent Karmapas.

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:52.310
The second Karmapa, Karma
Pakshi, was renowned

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:56.160
for his spiritual mastery,
which led to his invitation

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:57.893
to the court of Kublai Khan.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:02.090
The third Karmapa,
Rangjung Dorje, was famous

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:05.763
for his exegesis on philosophy
and esoteric Buddhist theory.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:09.450
And subsequent Karmapas became consummate

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:14.450
religious teachers, great
scholars, virtuoso meditators,

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:17.000
artists and poets.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:21.260
They were also teachers who
carried out the activities

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:25.543
of a Buddha, as the title Karmapa
is sometimes etymologized.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:31.970
The 16th Karmapa, Rangjung
Rigpe Dorje, fled Tibet in 1959

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:36.720
and established a monastic
seat in Sikkim, India.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.710
He is remembered for his
great love of animals,

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:42.290
especially birds, and also for his ability

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:43.603
to communicate with them.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:48.130
The 16th was part of an early
wave of Tibetan teachers

00:07:48.130 --> 00:07:51.370
who brought traditional
Buddhist teachings to the West.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.590
He visited the United States first in 1974

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:56.900
and again in 1977.

00:07:56.900 --> 00:07:59.060
And during this period he established

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:02.710
a major monastic center
outside Woodstock, New York,

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:05.620
which now has dozens of affiliated centers

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:06.633
around the world.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:13.340
Ogyen Trinley Dorje was born
in 1985 in Eastern Tibet,

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:16.030
and based upon his
predecessors prediction letter

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:19.470
and testament he was
recognized and then enthroned

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:22.540
as the 17th Karmapa at
the traditional monastery

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:26.070
of his lineage at
Tsurphu in Central Tibet.

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:28.950
And I was fortunate to meet
His Holiness numerous times

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:31.370
at Tsurphu, and it was clear even then

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:33.733
that he was an extraordinary individual.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.640
In 1999 he determined
that he was no longer able

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:42.780
to receive an adequate
religious education inside Tibet

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:46.230
and so, over the course of
eight days, in late December

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:49.700
and early January, he
undertook a harrowing journey

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:51.570
across the Himalayan range.

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:53.400
The story is quite dramatic.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:56.420
It includes a daring escape
through an upper storey window

00:08:56.420 --> 00:09:01.330
of the monastery, a trek
across frozen mountain passes

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:04.120
and horseback travel
through Northern Nepal.

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:07.520
He eventually reached Dharamsala in India,

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:09.760
where he maintains a close relationship

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:12.310
with another of Tibet's
great religious figures,

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:13.973
the 14th Dalai Lama.

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:19.120
At 29 years old, His Holiness
is already recognized

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:22.170
as a leading religious
figure of his generation,

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:24.740
as an accomplished Buddhist teacher.

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:29.600
But he is also a prolific
artist, poet and playwright.

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:34.260
He is a religious reformer
dedicated to gender equality

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:36.690
and the restoration of Bhikṣuṇī

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:39.083
or full monastic ordination for women.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:43.620
He's a social activist
committed to human rights,

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:47.680
vegetarianism and the
ethical treatment of animals.

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:51.550
He is an environmentalist
focused on the protection

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:54.800
of Himalayan landscapes
and natural resources

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:58.730
through an eco-monastic
program called Khoryug,

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:00.960
the Tibetan word for environment,

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.770
that networks more than
50 religious institutions

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:05.253
across the Himalaya.

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:10.997
In His Holiness's own
words, "Whatever it is I do,

00:10:10.997 --> 00:10:14.347
"I wanted to have a
long-term visible impact

00:10:14.347 --> 00:10:16.637
"and for it to be practical.

00:10:16.637 --> 00:10:20.037
"If I have the opportunity,
I would most like

00:10:20.037 --> 00:10:22.797
"to restore the natural
environment in the Himalayas

00:10:22.797 --> 00:10:26.467
"and Tibet and to especially
protect the forests,

00:10:26.467 --> 00:10:29.177
"the water and the
wildlife of the region."

00:10:30.393 --> 00:10:32.600
And I think it is not
an exaggeration to say

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:35.060
that His Holiness the 17th Karmapa

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:38.420
will revolutionize Buddhism, not only

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:41.573
in the Tibetan tradition
but also on a global scale.

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:46.040
The reach of the Karmapa's
activities is reflected

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:50.390
in the way that his visit
has brought people across...

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:54.370
Has brought people together
across Yale University,

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:57.520
from the Arts and
Sciences to Public Health

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:00.530
and Environmental Studies, faculty,

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:02.993
graduate students and undergraduates.

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:06.660
Some time ago, long before
His Holiness's visit

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:10.020
had been publicly announced,
one of the eight Tibetans

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:12.460
who works at Yale appeared in my office

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:15.817
and enthusiastically said, "I
read the Karmapa is coming!"

00:11:16.970 --> 00:11:17.803
Well, he was.

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:24.320
Today's address, entitled
Compassion in Action:

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:27.280
Buddhism and the Environment,
is the direct result

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:30.950
of converging interests, or
as Buddhists like to say,

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:35.080
of causes and conditions,
that span the University

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:37.360
and its wider community.

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:41.550
So the seed first
planted some 65 years ago

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:43.430
has now begun to flower.

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:46.530
So please join me now in
welcoming His Holiness

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:48.205
the 17th Karmapa.

00:11:48.205 --> 00:11:51.372
(audience applauding)

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:03.370
You want to stand next
to me or you wanna sit?

00:12:03.370 --> 00:12:04.607
It's just for a minute.

00:12:04.607 --> 00:12:07.774
(audience applauding)

00:12:28.803 --> 00:12:31.053
So thank you Your Holiness.

00:12:32.320 --> 00:12:34.330
So let me conclude with just a few words

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:35.823
about today's program.

00:12:37.090 --> 00:12:39.660
Following His Holiness's address,

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.970
he will be joined on stage by myself

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.660
and my colleague Mary Evelyn
Tucker for a short conversation

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:48.450
about the main themes
of the talk: compassion,

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:51.100
religion and the environment.

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:54.410
Mary Evelyn is a senior
lecturer and research scholar

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:56.200
at Yale University, where she teaches

00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:58.950
in a joint Master's degree
program between the School

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:01.040
of Forestry and Environmental Studies

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:03.120
and the Yale Divinity School.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:06.630
And she is also, and
perhaps more importantly,

00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:10.130
the director of the Forum
on Religion and Ecology

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:13.183
at Yale together with
her husband John Grim.

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:16.560
The program will conclude
with an opportunity

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:20.390
for His Holiness to address
questions from the audience.

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:23.020
Now, over the past week or
so, we've collected questions

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:26.410
by email from those who
would attend today's address

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:29.200
and we have received an
enthusiastic response

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:33.030
and have selected a
representative sample of them.

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:35.910
So I would be remiss if
I did not acknowledge

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:38.690
at least a few individuals
who were instrumental

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:41.790
in making His Holiness's
visit to Yale possible.

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:45.280
So my heartfelt thanks go, of course,

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:48.600
to the Chubb Fellowship,
to Master Jeff Brenzel

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:52.730
and Susan Wiggler, to George
Joseph of the Macmillan Center

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:57.180
for International and Area
Studies, and, of course,

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:00.470
to the incredible staff,
the indefatigable staff

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:02.990
of the Yale Himalaya Initiative,

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:06.578
including program director Alark Saxena,

00:14:06.578 --> 00:14:10.640
Anobha Gurung, Priyankar
Chand and Sangye Dorje.

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:12.540
So Your Holiness.

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:14.170
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

00:14:33.199 --> 00:14:37.116
(speaking in foreign language)

00:14:46.220 --> 00:14:48.120
- [Interpreter] First
I'd like to welcome each

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:49.544
and every one of you.

00:14:49.544 --> 00:14:51.510
Tashi Delek.

00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:55.427
(speaking in foreign language)

00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:01.760
- Tashi Delek means may you be filled

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:04.693
with auspicious goodness and joy.

00:15:10.117 --> 00:15:14.034
(speaking in foreign language)

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:43.590
- And I would also like to thank
everyone at Yale University

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:48.400
who has been responsible for inviting me

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:51.023
and giving me this opportunity to be here.

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:56.397
(speaking in foreign language)

00:16:03.089 --> 00:16:04.960
- And I would also like
to thank all of you

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:06.463
who have come here today.

00:16:09.532 --> 00:16:13.449
(speaking in foreign language)

00:16:15.460 --> 00:16:16.804
(coughing)

00:16:16.804 --> 00:16:20.721
(speaking in foreign language)

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:17.350
- This is my third visit to
the United States of America,

00:17:17.350 --> 00:17:21.560
and whenever I come here I have
a special feeling of freedom

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:26.290
and joy, especially this
time, the third time,

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:29.340
as I've been visiting
several universities.

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:33.720
I've been overjoyed by
the experience of meeting

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:37.150
with many students and
witnessing a little bit

00:17:37.150 --> 00:17:39.523
of their study and university life.

00:17:43.634 --> 00:17:44.467
(coughs)

00:17:44.467 --> 00:17:48.384
(speaking in foreign language)

00:17:55.510 --> 00:17:59.560
- Now, the topic you've asked
me to address is compassion

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:00.973
and the environment.

00:18:08.181 --> 00:18:12.098
(speaking in foreign language)

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:38.490
- To give you a little bit of background,

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:42.487
many people have asked
me, "What inspired you

00:18:42.487 --> 00:18:45.580
"to become so concerned
about the environment?"

00:18:45.580 --> 00:18:49.213
So I'm gonna talk a little
bit about that to begin.

00:18:53.937 --> 00:18:57.854
(speaking in foreign language)

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:21.940
- I was born in a quite
isolated part of Eastern Tibet,

00:20:22.870 --> 00:20:27.030
and where I was born there had been

00:20:27.030 --> 00:20:30.733
no great modern development,

00:20:31.750 --> 00:20:35.070
so I lived in a very natural

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:37.870
and somewhat pristine environment.

00:20:37.870 --> 00:20:41.160
Until I was seven, this
was the only environment

00:20:41.160 --> 00:20:42.540
in which I lived.

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:46.910
So I experienced firsthand
as a child a feeling

00:20:46.910 --> 00:20:51.290
of intimate connection with
the natural environment,

00:20:51.290 --> 00:20:55.550
and I retained to this day a constant

00:20:55.550 --> 00:20:58.823
and extremely clear recollection of this.

00:21:00.039 --> 00:21:03.956
(speaking in foreign language)

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:55.320
- For example, where
I was born we regarded

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:57.890
and experienced our environment

00:22:57.890 --> 00:23:02.890
as a living system, living beings.

00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:08.160
The mountains, the sources
of water were all regarded

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:12.580
as the dwelling places of
what I would call holy spirits

00:23:12.580 --> 00:23:13.883
of various kinds.

00:23:15.020 --> 00:23:20.020
We therefore respected every
aspect of the environment

00:23:20.150 --> 00:23:22.800
as part of a living system.

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:25.910
We didn't wash our
clothes or even our hands

00:23:25.910 --> 00:23:29.630
in a flowing water sources.

00:23:29.630 --> 00:23:33.120
We didn't cast any kind of garbage

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:38.003
or any kind of other pollutant
into the fire in our hearth.

00:23:38.920 --> 00:23:43.920
So we regarded the entire
environment as innately sacred.

00:23:46.936 --> 00:23:50.853
(speaking in foreign language)

00:24:49.230 --> 00:24:52.990
- One of the challenges
that those who live

00:24:52.990 --> 00:24:56.840
in such an environment
face nowadays is the use

00:24:56.840 --> 00:25:01.840
of artificial and non
biodegradable substances,

00:25:03.150 --> 00:25:07.240
because for the first
time they have to deal

00:25:07.240 --> 00:25:09.050
with real garbage.

00:25:09.050 --> 00:25:10.750
We didn't really have garbage

00:25:10.750 --> 00:25:13.500
because we used pretty well everything up,

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:15.560
and anything that we did dispose of

00:25:15.560 --> 00:25:18.440
was easily biodegradable.

00:25:18.440 --> 00:25:21.960
And now, because of the
introduction of artificial materials

00:25:21.960 --> 00:25:25.580
that are not biodegradable,
we have to come up

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:28.440
with new ways to dispose of them.

00:25:28.440 --> 00:25:32.520
And so this, to give you one example,

00:25:32.520 --> 00:25:36.677
is changing the lifestyle
of people in such places.

00:25:38.927 --> 00:25:42.844
(speaking in foreign language)

00:26:21.690 --> 00:26:25.670
- Obviously, as a child, I
had no knowledge of philosophy

00:26:25.670 --> 00:26:30.670
or science, but I had a strong
emotional connection with

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:34.380
and strong feelings for the environment,

00:26:34.380 --> 00:26:37.490
which means that now that I've
begun to acquire a little bit

00:26:37.490 --> 00:26:40.810
of knowledge, it isn't
just knowledge for me,

00:26:40.810 --> 00:26:42.603
it's based on my strong feelings.

00:26:54.823 --> 00:26:58.740
(speaking in foreign language)

00:27:40.830 --> 00:27:45.830
- I think that in order to
understand the necessity

00:27:46.440 --> 00:27:50.590
of environmental protection,
we need to understand

00:27:50.590 --> 00:27:55.590
how connected we are to one
another and to our environment.

00:27:56.980 --> 00:28:00.960
So, in order to understand
our connectedness,

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:04.383
I think we need to
understand interdependence.

00:28:05.843 --> 00:28:09.760
(speaking in foreign language)

00:28:31.174 --> 00:28:32.220
- Lots of echo.

00:28:32.220 --> 00:28:36.840
- Yeah, I'm having a really
hard time hearing, I'm sorry.

00:28:36.840 --> 00:28:37.720
- What do we do?

00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:38.553
- I don't know.

00:28:38.553 --> 00:28:40.140
Maybe if I move back.

00:28:40.140 --> 00:28:40.973
- Really?

00:28:42.300 --> 00:28:43.200
- Might be better.

00:28:45.834 --> 00:28:47.075
(mumbling)

00:28:47.075 --> 00:28:49.180
(audience laughing)

00:28:49.180 --> 00:28:51.673
- Maybe you can sit down?

00:28:51.673 --> 00:28:52.506
- No.

00:28:52.506 --> 00:28:54.433
(mumbling)

00:28:54.433 --> 00:28:57.467
(laughing)

00:28:57.467 --> 00:28:59.717
(mumbling)

00:29:02.348 --> 00:29:06.265
(speaking in foreign language)

00:29:33.490 --> 00:29:38.490
- For example, for us to
acquire or eat any food,

00:29:38.610 --> 00:29:43.610
have clothes to wear or even
to have the bodies we do,

00:29:43.830 --> 00:29:48.400
all of these things require
the interconnectedness

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:52.160
of many aspects of many
things and many people

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:53.383
within the environment.

00:29:55.778 --> 00:29:59.695
(speaking in foreign language)

00:30:39.720 --> 00:30:44.100
- The value of understanding
interdependence in this regard

00:30:44.100 --> 00:30:49.100
is that we often feel at some
distance from our environment.

00:30:50.220 --> 00:30:54.600
We divide the world into
subject and object and we feel

00:30:54.600 --> 00:30:58.470
that the external environment is an object

00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:01.150
separated from us by some kind of boundary

00:31:01.150 --> 00:31:05.520
and at some distance from we,
from ourselves as subjects.

00:31:05.520 --> 00:31:10.030
We need to dissolve
this artificial boundary

00:31:10.030 --> 00:31:13.190
and decrease the distance
between ourselves

00:31:13.190 --> 00:31:14.393
and our environment.

00:31:16.687 --> 00:31:20.604
(speaking in foreign language)

00:31:33.550 --> 00:31:36.440
- And I think if we do that we can begin

00:31:36.440 --> 00:31:40.350
to feel how connected we
are to our environment

00:31:40.350 --> 00:31:42.830
and how little distance, if any,

00:31:42.830 --> 00:31:46.253
there really is between
ourselves and our environment.

00:31:47.473 --> 00:31:51.390
(speaking in foreign language)

00:32:49.070 --> 00:32:52.420
- In Tibetan we often
refer to the relationship

00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:55.220
between the external
environment and the beings

00:32:55.220 --> 00:32:58.270
who inhabit that environment
as the relationship

00:32:58.270 --> 00:33:01.810
between a container and its contents.

00:33:01.810 --> 00:33:06.810
We are all held by and within
the environment we inhabit.

00:33:06.810 --> 00:33:09.650
We are like the contents
or even the nutrients

00:33:09.650 --> 00:33:11.810
within such an environment,

00:33:11.810 --> 00:33:15.020
and it seems that it's
necessary for us to begin

00:33:15.020 --> 00:33:18.633
to acknowledge that interrelationship.

00:33:24.441 --> 00:33:28.358
(speaking in foreign language)

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:08.200
- We therefore also need
to acknowledge the fact

00:34:08.200 --> 00:34:11.900
that our environment can affect us

00:34:11.900 --> 00:34:15.430
and we can also affect our environment.

00:34:15.430 --> 00:34:19.200
Some people have the idea that
the environment is so vast

00:34:19.200 --> 00:34:22.720
and so primordial that nothing
we do is actually going

00:34:22.720 --> 00:34:23.750
to affect it.

00:34:23.750 --> 00:34:27.170
Unfortunately, that's been
proven not to be the case,

00:34:27.170 --> 00:34:32.017
and we need to begin to
acknowledge the interdependence,

00:34:33.030 --> 00:34:35.050
that the aspect of interdependence,

00:34:35.050 --> 00:34:37.750
that is our effect on the environment,

00:34:37.750 --> 00:34:40.503
even as our environment affects us.

00:34:42.972 --> 00:34:46.889
(speaking in foreign language)

00:35:50.950 --> 00:35:53.130
- Well, we need to
acknowledge these things.

00:35:53.130 --> 00:35:56.200
Mere knowledge or
understanding alone, of course,

00:35:56.200 --> 00:35:57.253
is not enough.

00:35:58.370 --> 00:36:02.920
One of our problems is that
we tend to separate ourselves

00:36:02.920 --> 00:36:06.683
as persons from our
knowledge or understanding.

00:36:07.930 --> 00:36:11.260
And if we understand
or know about the need

00:36:11.260 --> 00:36:14.310
for environmental protection,
but do not apply it

00:36:14.310 --> 00:36:18.740
in our daily lives, we're not
going to achieve anything.

00:36:18.740 --> 00:36:22.690
Our knowledge or understanding
has to become so intimate,

00:36:22.690 --> 00:36:27.690
so personal to us, that it
naturally changes our behavior,

00:36:28.530 --> 00:36:31.923
that we feel it emotionally
and in our hearts.

00:36:35.228 --> 00:36:39.145
(speaking in foreign language)

00:36:56.345 --> 00:36:58.430
- We need therefore to
reflect upon the fact

00:36:58.430 --> 00:37:03.430
that our knowledge of the
environment really is a knowledge

00:37:05.370 --> 00:37:07.900
of something that's going
to affect our lives,

00:37:07.900 --> 00:37:10.680
a knowledge of something that is going

00:37:10.680 --> 00:37:12.723
to affect our well-being.

00:37:14.288 --> 00:37:18.205
(speaking in foreign language)

00:37:33.800 --> 00:37:36.470
- And I think it is in
that regard that spiritual

00:37:36.470 --> 00:37:39.610
and religious traditions
can serve the cause

00:37:39.610 --> 00:37:41.333
of environmental stewardship.

00:37:43.198 --> 00:37:47.115
(speaking in foreign language)

00:38:46.220 --> 00:38:47.950
- And when I say religious traditions,

00:38:47.950 --> 00:38:50.020
I don't just mean Buddhist traditions,

00:38:50.020 --> 00:38:53.390
I mean every religious
tradition in this world

00:38:53.390 --> 00:38:58.390
can play its part, and I'm
confident that if we do this,

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:04.200
if we work together, if
we apply the knowledge

00:39:04.200 --> 00:39:08.070
that is embodied in the
many indigenous cultures

00:39:08.070 --> 00:39:13.070
that have survived, the ancient
wisdom of our ancestors,

00:39:13.250 --> 00:39:18.020
we will be able to discover together

00:39:18.020 --> 00:39:22.160
that environmental protection
is not just a point of view,

00:39:22.160 --> 00:39:26.533
not just science, but
must be a way of life.

00:39:29.373 --> 00:39:33.290
(speaking in foreign language)

00:40:37.730 --> 00:40:40.950
- At various times in our history, science

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:45.890
and religion have seemed to be
contradictory to one another,

00:40:45.890 --> 00:40:49.640
have even become enemies,
attacked one another, or,

00:40:49.640 --> 00:40:51.913
to be more polite, refuted one another.

00:40:52.810 --> 00:40:56.680
But really there is a great
commonality of experience

00:40:56.680 --> 00:40:57.700
that we all share.

00:40:57.700 --> 00:41:01.150
We're all human beings
living in the same world,

00:41:01.150 --> 00:41:03.590
relying on the same environment.

00:41:03.590 --> 00:41:07.680
And therefore, on that basis, on the basis

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:11.790
of acknowledging our shared
or common experience,

00:41:11.790 --> 00:41:14.990
we have to accept that the
protection of this environment

00:41:14.990 --> 00:41:19.163
is beyond the views of any one religion.

00:41:22.920 --> 00:41:26.837
(speaking in foreign language)

00:42:22.270 --> 00:42:26.710
- If we base our approach
to environmental stewardship

00:42:26.710 --> 00:42:29.863
on being loving and compassionate,

00:42:30.780 --> 00:42:32.770
there should be no contradiction

00:42:32.770 --> 00:42:35.363
between science and spirituality.

00:42:36.650 --> 00:42:41.650
We can share, undertake and
share our common responsibility

00:42:42.940 --> 00:42:47.940
for this environment, and
in that way actually put

00:42:48.350 --> 00:42:53.350
into practice and make
practical use of the information

00:42:54.430 --> 00:42:57.700
that scientists give us
about the environment,

00:42:57.700 --> 00:43:01.413
and not live as though
we are in denial of it.

00:43:05.131 --> 00:43:09.048
(speaking in foreign language)

00:44:15.250 --> 00:44:18.690
- The Himalaya, including the Tibetan...

00:44:18.690 --> 00:44:23.690
And the Tibetan plateau are
considered a treasury of water

00:44:24.960 --> 00:44:28.140
that feed the great rivers of Asia

00:44:28.140 --> 00:44:31.000
and therefore supply so much water

00:44:31.000 --> 00:44:36.000
that the, sometimes, the
Himalaya is called a third Pole.

00:44:37.280 --> 00:44:39.900
Therefore we have to
be especially concerned

00:44:39.900 --> 00:44:44.090
with the environment in the Himalaya

00:44:44.090 --> 00:44:47.350
and conservation of water there.

00:44:47.350 --> 00:44:52.350
So what we have tried to do is
train monasteries in best...

00:44:53.130 --> 00:44:57.200
Himalayan monasteries in
best environmental practices

00:44:57.200 --> 00:45:00.800
so that they can then
present these to their local

00:45:00.800 --> 00:45:02.313
or neighboring communities.

00:45:03.776 --> 00:45:07.693
(speaking in foreign language)

00:45:58.890 --> 00:46:02.190
- When this subject was first
introduced to representatives

00:46:02.190 --> 00:46:04.290
of these monasteries,
they were immediately

00:46:04.290 --> 00:46:08.270
emotionally affected and
demonstrated tremendous commitment

00:46:08.270 --> 00:46:11.740
to environmental
stewardship in their region,

00:46:11.740 --> 00:46:16.480
but at the same time we
lack a scientific knowledge

00:46:16.480 --> 00:46:18.960
and technical expertise.

00:46:18.960 --> 00:46:22.600
So in order to do this we need the support

00:46:22.600 --> 00:46:25.150
of the scientific community
and the assistance

00:46:25.150 --> 00:46:27.650
of the scientific community.

00:46:27.650 --> 00:46:30.340
And I mentioned this as an example of how,

00:46:30.340 --> 00:46:33.350
in order to preserve our environment,

00:46:33.350 --> 00:46:35.320
we all need to work together

00:46:35.320 --> 00:46:38.253
and offer our individual
skills and knowledge.

00:46:39.877 --> 00:46:43.794
(speaking in foreign language)

00:47:00.520 --> 00:47:05.520
- I'm going to end my initial
address or talk with that

00:47:06.350 --> 00:47:10.500
and we'll begin the conversation.

00:47:10.500 --> 00:47:13.400
Before we do, however, I
again want to thank each

00:47:13.400 --> 00:47:15.963
and every one of you for coming here,

00:47:17.557 --> 00:47:20.030
and convey to you my sincere delight

00:47:20.030 --> 00:47:22.340
in having this opportunity.

00:47:22.340 --> 00:47:23.450
- Thank you.

00:47:23.450 --> 00:47:24.477
Thank you.

00:47:24.477 --> 00:47:26.204
(audience applauding)

00:47:26.204 --> 00:47:27.037
(coughs)

00:47:27.037 --> 00:47:30.204
(audience applauding)

00:47:32.274 --> 00:47:33.274
- Thank you.

00:47:35.963 --> 00:47:40.120
- [Andrew] (mumbling) glass of water.

00:47:40.120 --> 00:47:43.390
- Thank you Your Holiness,
and thank you translator.

00:47:43.390 --> 00:47:45.040
- Yeah.

00:47:45.040 --> 00:47:49.260
- We wanted to begin by
picking up on a point

00:47:49.260 --> 00:47:51.670
that you've made earlier--

00:47:51.670 --> 00:47:52.503
- Yes.

00:47:52.503 --> 00:47:57.503
- About the route of compassion
is having a steadfast heart,

00:47:57.660 --> 00:47:59.240
the power of the heart.

00:47:59.240 --> 00:48:01.050
And this work with the environment

00:48:01.050 --> 00:48:03.620
for many people can be discouraging

00:48:03.620 --> 00:48:06.300
because it's so overwhelming right now.

00:48:06.300 --> 00:48:09.960
So could you speak about this
need for a steadfast heart,

00:48:09.960 --> 00:48:13.048
the power of the heart in compassion?

00:48:13.048 --> 00:48:13.881
- Mm-hm.

00:48:19.489 --> 00:48:23.406
(speaking in foreign language)

00:48:59.060 --> 00:49:02.440
- Well, first of all, I would
say that many activists,

00:49:02.440 --> 00:49:05.690
and especially environmental activists,

00:49:05.690 --> 00:49:08.880
naturally become highly
emotionally invested

00:49:08.880 --> 00:49:10.210
in their activism.

00:49:10.210 --> 00:49:13.470
And when after years and
years of very hard work

00:49:13.470 --> 00:49:15.770
they don't see a great deal of result,

00:49:15.770 --> 00:49:19.833
they can become a little
bit embittered and angry.

00:49:20.996 --> 00:49:22.377
(clears throat)

00:49:22.377 --> 00:49:26.294
(speaking in foreign language)

00:49:37.558 --> 00:49:40.641
(interpreter laughs)

00:49:42.399 --> 00:49:45.230
- And if you spend enough time listening

00:49:45.230 --> 00:49:48.290
to one dire prediction about the future

00:49:48.290 --> 00:49:51.920
of this planet after another
from environmental scientists,

00:49:51.920 --> 00:49:56.500
even we listeners can develop a little bit

00:49:56.500 --> 00:49:58.477
of a problem in our brains.

00:49:58.477 --> 00:49:59.830
(Mary laughs)

00:49:59.830 --> 00:50:00.663
- Yeah.

00:50:01.874 --> 00:50:05.791
(speaking in foreign language)

00:50:22.514 --> 00:50:27.040
- I think there are two
levels or types of activism,

00:50:27.040 --> 00:50:31.370
and the one is what we all practice.

00:50:31.370 --> 00:50:35.440
We can practice it by being supportive

00:50:35.440 --> 00:50:39.987
of intensive activism, but
even if we are in denial

00:50:41.470 --> 00:50:43.950
of the need for environmental activism,

00:50:43.950 --> 00:50:46.730
because we're having an
effect on the environment,

00:50:46.730 --> 00:50:49.823
you can call it a type of mirror activism.

00:50:50.943 --> 00:50:54.860
(speaking in foreign language)

00:51:40.480 --> 00:51:43.460
- The other type of activist
is what we would normally refer

00:51:43.460 --> 00:51:45.490
to as a non environmental activist.

00:51:45.490 --> 00:51:49.840
And that's someone who
intentionally undertakes

00:51:49.840 --> 00:51:52.990
responsibility and puts great
effort into the achievement

00:51:52.990 --> 00:51:56.600
of, in this case, environmental
goals and changes.

00:51:56.600 --> 00:52:00.180
I think that one thing
that's very important is

00:52:00.180 --> 00:52:04.370
for those of us who are
less directly involved

00:52:04.370 --> 00:52:08.100
to take some responsibility
and be supportive

00:52:08.100 --> 00:52:11.190
as much as we can of
environmental activism,

00:52:11.190 --> 00:52:13.580
because if you look at the situation,

00:52:13.580 --> 00:52:15.790
there is absolutely no reason not

00:52:15.790 --> 00:52:19.111
to support environmental activism.

00:52:19.111 --> 00:52:23.028
(speaking in foreign language)

00:52:59.760 --> 00:53:03.650
- To use myself as an
example, I'm a vegetarian.

00:53:03.650 --> 00:53:06.930
And becoming vegetarian,
which in my case is partly

00:53:06.930 --> 00:53:09.960
for environmental reasons, is something

00:53:09.960 --> 00:53:11.410
that anyone can do.

00:53:11.410 --> 00:53:13.200
It doesn't require an organization,

00:53:13.200 --> 00:53:16.560
it doesn't require extensive
scientific knowledge

00:53:16.560 --> 00:53:18.030
or anything like that.

00:53:18.030 --> 00:53:21.020
But, nevertheless, as simple as it is,

00:53:21.020 --> 00:53:23.610
when you do that, when you
make that kind of change

00:53:23.610 --> 00:53:27.450
in your life, it's very
encouraging, because you realize

00:53:27.450 --> 00:53:30.000
that you're not without power,

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:32.570
you have the ability, even as one person,

00:53:32.570 --> 00:53:35.130
to do at least something
that is beneficial

00:53:35.130 --> 00:53:36.861
to the environment.

00:53:36.861 --> 00:53:40.778
(speaking in foreign language)

00:53:48.850 --> 00:53:52.040
- I think that the greatest
source of courage for us

00:53:52.040 --> 00:53:53.840
in environmental stewardship comes

00:53:53.840 --> 00:53:57.580
from making such simple
changes in our lives

00:53:57.580 --> 00:54:01.002
and realizing how much power we do have.

00:54:01.002 --> 00:54:01.835
- Yes.

00:54:01.835 --> 00:54:02.668
- [Andrew] Thank you.

00:54:02.668 --> 00:54:03.501
- [Mary] Thank you.

00:54:03.501 --> 00:54:06.253
- So Your Holiness, just to
follow up on that last point,

00:54:07.950 --> 00:54:09.960
you raised the question
in The Heart is Noble,

00:54:09.960 --> 00:54:13.127
where you said, "What
does it take for our ideas

00:54:13.127 --> 00:54:17.607
"to move our heart so that
is what is it that moves us

00:54:17.607 --> 00:54:20.957
"to overcome our habits and
desires like meat-eating

00:54:20.957 --> 00:54:23.080
"or mass consumerism?"

00:54:23.080 --> 00:54:24.910
So I wonder if you could say a little bit

00:54:24.910 --> 00:54:29.250
about what it means to
move past good intentions

00:54:29.250 --> 00:54:31.123
and move into actual action.

00:54:34.650 --> 00:54:36.173
- Lots of echo, actually.

00:54:37.234 --> 00:54:41.151
(speaking in foreign language)

00:55:00.933 --> 00:55:04.010
- Oh, so consumerism
are you talking about?

00:55:04.010 --> 00:55:04.843
- Yes.
- Sure.

00:55:04.843 --> 00:55:05.963
- Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:55:08.499 --> 00:55:12.416
(speaking in foreign language)

00:55:15.270 --> 00:55:17.480
- You mean how to bridge--
- Yeah.

00:55:17.480 --> 00:55:19.130
- Good intentions into...

00:55:20.228 --> 00:55:24.145
(speaking in foreign language)

00:55:48.050 --> 00:55:50.500
- Actually, you can repeat that, I guess?

00:55:50.500 --> 00:55:51.640
- [Andrew] Sure.

00:55:51.640 --> 00:55:52.473
- I'm sorry.

00:55:52.473 --> 00:55:54.980
- Your Holiness, you mentioned yourself

00:55:54.980 --> 00:55:57.490
you've written before and
in talks that you've given

00:55:57.490 --> 00:55:59.670
that when you were quite
young you were fond

00:55:59.670 --> 00:56:02.380
of eating meat but you
had the good intention

00:56:02.380 --> 00:56:03.260
of giving that up.

00:56:03.260 --> 00:56:04.320
You're now--
- Yeah.

00:56:04.320 --> 00:56:05.370
- A vegetarian
- Yes.

00:56:05.370 --> 00:56:07.200
- And that is taking a good
intention and putting it into--

00:56:07.200 --> 00:56:08.623
- Still I like meat.

00:56:09.610 --> 00:56:10.820
But it's okay.

00:56:10.820 --> 00:56:14.010
- But many people wonder
how to take a good intention

00:56:14.010 --> 00:56:16.490
and to actually put it into practice.

00:56:16.490 --> 00:56:17.393
- Okay.

00:56:18.546 --> 00:56:21.516
(audience laughing)

00:56:21.516 --> 00:56:23.802
Put it into practice.

00:56:23.802 --> 00:56:27.719
(speaking in foreign language)

00:57:10.270 --> 00:57:13.960
- Well, to use the example you
brought up, my vegetarianism,

00:57:13.960 --> 00:57:16.770
as you mentioned, as a child I ate meat.

00:57:16.770 --> 00:57:20.290
In fact, I only stopped eating
meat about eight years ago.

00:57:20.290 --> 00:57:24.780
So I kept on eating meat for many years,

00:57:24.780 --> 00:57:26.490
but I knew it was wrong.

00:57:26.490 --> 00:57:27.393
I still ate it.

00:57:29.314 --> 00:57:33.231
(speaking in foreign language)

00:58:16.246 --> 00:58:19.321
- In that type of case, I think that...

00:58:19.321 --> 00:58:23.821
The bridge is a type of
compassion, a type of courage,

00:58:24.960 --> 00:58:28.060
the power of your heart.

00:58:28.060 --> 00:58:30.530
And by the power of your heart I mean that

00:58:30.530 --> 00:58:34.040
which enables us to
decide once and for all

00:58:34.040 --> 00:58:37.360
to take responsibility for our own actions

00:58:37.360 --> 00:58:42.360
as individuals, to be able to
commit ourselves to accepting

00:58:42.480 --> 00:58:46.703
and fulfilling our
responsibilities as a person.

00:58:47.661 --> 00:58:51.578
(speaking in foreign language)

00:59:15.430 --> 00:59:19.040
- So the bridge is that
a mere understanding

00:59:19.040 --> 00:59:22.690
become more emotionally felt,

00:59:22.690 --> 00:59:27.410
and a deep emotional feeling
based on understanding

00:59:27.410 --> 00:59:29.690
will inspire us to take action

00:59:30.660 --> 00:59:35.660
once we listen to the
advice of our hearts.

00:59:36.880 --> 00:59:38.463
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

00:59:40.000 --> 00:59:43.730
- You spoke on science and religion,

00:59:43.730 --> 00:59:47.740
a very important topic,
a very complex topic.

00:59:47.740 --> 00:59:50.430
I'd love you to speak a little bit more

00:59:50.430 --> 00:59:52.558
because for many years--

00:59:52.558 --> 00:59:53.391
- Mm-hm.

00:59:53.391 --> 00:59:55.070
- Scientists have been speaking--

00:59:55.070 --> 00:59:56.530
- Yeah.
- About climate change

00:59:56.530 --> 00:59:58.050
and the environment.

00:59:58.050 --> 01:00:03.050
And now there's a new moment
when some scientists are saying

01:00:04.390 --> 01:00:09.390
this is no longer an
issue of science alone

01:00:09.390 --> 01:00:10.730
but a moral issue.

01:00:10.730 --> 01:00:11.700
- Mm-hm.

01:00:11.700 --> 01:00:14.350
- And that's what your work encourages us

01:00:14.350 --> 01:00:17.810
in this new dialogue of
science and religion.

01:00:17.810 --> 01:00:19.093
Can you speak on that?

01:00:28.457 --> 01:00:32.374
(speaking in foreign language)

01:03:33.558 --> 01:03:36.550
(interpreter sighs)

01:03:36.550 --> 01:03:41.550
- As you said, environmental
protection or conservation

01:03:44.200 --> 01:03:47.083
is fundamentally a moral issue.

01:03:49.010 --> 01:03:54.010
And the reason, the founding,
the crucial factor in it

01:03:57.340 --> 01:04:00.940
that makes it a moral issue
is that the degradation

01:04:00.940 --> 01:04:03.790
or destruction of the
environment has been caused

01:04:03.790 --> 01:04:05.243
by human greed.

01:04:06.610 --> 01:04:11.560
And our human greed, bad
enough as it is to start with,

01:04:11.560 --> 01:04:16.560
has only been exacerbated
and fed by our media

01:04:16.730 --> 01:04:18.963
and our advertising industry.

01:04:20.410 --> 01:04:25.410
The problem we face therefore
is that human desire

01:04:25.420 --> 01:04:30.420
is limitless, but environmental
resources are limited.

01:04:33.220 --> 01:04:37.130
And therefore it is our
responsibility as those

01:04:37.130 --> 01:04:41.270
who depend on these resources to rein in

01:04:41.270 --> 01:04:43.253
and control our greed.

01:04:46.170 --> 01:04:50.550
In order that we start
doing so it is essential

01:04:50.550 --> 01:04:53.060
that religious leaders, spiritual leaders,

01:04:53.060 --> 01:04:56.200
spiritual teachers, not only preach

01:04:56.200 --> 01:04:58.640
about individual practice issues

01:04:58.640 --> 01:05:03.390
or individual moral codes
but also about global issues

01:05:03.390 --> 01:05:07.500
and that they provide moral guidance

01:05:08.540 --> 01:05:12.100
on environmental stewardship,

01:05:12.100 --> 01:05:17.100
because they are the ones
who can evoke feeling,

01:05:17.950 --> 01:05:21.960
evoke enthusiasm, evoke
an emotional commitment

01:05:21.960 --> 01:05:23.123
in their followers.

01:05:24.360 --> 01:05:27.080
Until we accept the fact
that our environment

01:05:27.080 --> 01:05:29.960
is not external to us, it pervades us,

01:05:29.960 --> 01:05:33.200
it's within our bodies
and our minds as well,

01:05:33.200 --> 01:05:36.913
it will be difficult for
us to change our behavior.

01:05:37.860 --> 01:05:42.860
But the key to becoming really motivated,

01:05:44.050 --> 01:05:49.050
to change the way we
live, and thereby begin

01:05:49.440 --> 01:05:53.280
to heal the environment, the
key that will open the door

01:05:53.280 --> 01:05:58.060
to that type of motivation,
seems to be in the hands

01:05:58.060 --> 01:06:02.460
of those religious and spiritual
leaders who are willing

01:06:02.460 --> 01:06:06.520
to provide moral guidance
concerning the environment

01:06:06.520 --> 01:06:09.730
that is based on sound
scientific knowledge.

01:06:09.730 --> 01:06:10.563
- [Andrew] Thank you.

01:06:10.563 --> 01:06:11.430
- Thank you.

01:06:11.430 --> 01:06:12.270
- Thank you.

01:06:12.270 --> 01:06:13.660
So to take the conversation

01:06:13.660 --> 01:06:15.480
in a slightly different direction,

01:06:15.480 --> 01:06:18.990
Your Holiness, you
spoke about the practice

01:06:18.990 --> 01:06:22.490
of dissolving the distinction
between subject and object,

01:06:22.490 --> 01:06:24.590
the inner and outer worlds.

01:06:24.590 --> 01:06:27.500
And, of course, there are many techniques

01:06:27.500 --> 01:06:29.480
for putting that into
practice in meditation.

01:06:29.480 --> 01:06:33.900
We can study about that,
you can practice it,

01:06:33.900 --> 01:06:37.700
but I wonder, does the
practice of cultivating,

01:06:37.700 --> 01:06:41.070
of dissolving that
distinction naturally lead

01:06:41.070 --> 01:06:43.790
to a feeling of connection
for the environment,

01:06:43.790 --> 01:06:46.270
for an appreciation of the environment?

01:06:46.270 --> 01:06:47.103
- No.

01:06:48.470 --> 01:06:49.303
Appreciation.

01:06:53.442 --> 01:06:57.359
(speaking in foreign language)

01:08:20.959 --> 01:08:24.750
- We have a number of
meditation practices,

01:08:24.750 --> 01:08:26.870
as you mentioned in
your question, concerned

01:08:26.870 --> 01:08:29.710
with dissolvering, dissolving, excuse me,

01:08:29.710 --> 01:08:34.680
the false boundary between self and other.

01:08:34.680 --> 01:08:39.460
One of these is contemplating
the equality of oneself

01:08:39.460 --> 01:08:42.720
and others, which is
fundamentally developing

01:08:42.720 --> 01:08:47.720
an empathetic recognition that
just as I want to be happy

01:08:49.310 --> 01:08:52.140
and not suffer so do others,

01:08:52.140 --> 01:08:55.180
and that in that we're exactly the same.

01:08:55.180 --> 01:08:57.520
And once you have taken this to heart,

01:08:57.520 --> 01:09:00.040
then of course you have
to put it into practice

01:09:00.040 --> 01:09:01.940
and live accordingly.

01:09:01.940 --> 01:09:06.940
We also do meditations on
exchanging one's self for others,

01:09:07.140 --> 01:09:10.730
where you actually imagine
yourself in the position

01:09:10.730 --> 01:09:14.180
of another and that other
in the position of yourself,

01:09:14.180 --> 01:09:19.180
and you try to imagine as
wholeheartedly as 100% as possible

01:09:19.900 --> 01:09:23.500
how they feel, what they're going through,

01:09:23.500 --> 01:09:25.300
and then apply that.

01:09:25.300 --> 01:09:29.120
So there should be ways to
extrapolate from these techniques

01:09:29.120 --> 01:09:32.555
and apply them to environmental awareness.

01:09:32.555 --> 01:09:36.472
(speaking in foreign language)

01:10:27.295 --> 01:10:31.712
- For example, if we think
about the fact that trees,

01:10:32.990 --> 01:10:36.850
so to speak, breathe out,
what we need to breathe in,

01:10:36.850 --> 01:10:40.470
oxygen, and we breathe out carbon dioxide,

01:10:40.470 --> 01:10:43.830
which they seem to need to,
so to speak, breathe in,

01:10:43.830 --> 01:10:48.230
so that our exhaust is their air

01:10:48.230 --> 01:10:51.510
and their exhaust is our air, it's...

01:10:51.510 --> 01:10:55.090
This is something we don't need
to imagine, this is a fact.

01:10:55.090 --> 01:10:59.730
So, if you contemplate that,
just the interrelationship

01:10:59.730 --> 01:11:03.530
or interconnectedness of
breathing, of our breathing

01:11:03.530 --> 01:11:06.690
and the vegetation, things that grow,

01:11:06.690 --> 01:11:10.260
that could be a meditation
technique in and of itself,

01:11:10.260 --> 01:11:12.100
because if you feel that, you realize

01:11:12.100 --> 01:11:14.700
that we're really two
parts of one whole machine

01:11:14.700 --> 01:11:15.883
or one whole system.

01:11:20.220 --> 01:11:23.500
- We wanted to give a
chance for a few questions

01:11:23.500 --> 01:11:26.990
from the audience which
were submitted in advance.

01:11:26.990 --> 01:11:28.700
So maybe two.

01:11:28.700 --> 01:11:29.533
- Yeah, two.

01:11:29.533 --> 01:11:30.840
- I look at some very good questions.

01:11:30.840 --> 01:11:32.460
- Thank you.

01:11:32.460 --> 01:11:35.870
- The first one is on climate change.

01:11:35.870 --> 01:11:37.420
- Mm-hm.

01:11:37.420 --> 01:11:40.280
- Climate change or global warming appears

01:11:40.280 --> 01:11:43.480
to be the world's greatest crisis,

01:11:43.480 --> 01:11:48.217
but there are many people who
deny global warming exists.

01:11:49.410 --> 01:11:53.160
What advice would you give
to working with people

01:11:53.160 --> 01:11:57.060
in the government who
believe there is no need

01:11:57.060 --> 01:11:59.063
to protect the environment?

01:12:00.572 --> 01:12:01.656
(sighing)

01:12:01.656 --> 01:12:03.906
(laughing)

01:12:07.010 --> 01:12:09.011
- I don't have all the solutions.

01:12:09.011 --> 01:12:11.261
(laughing)

01:12:12.257 --> 01:12:15.304
(audience applauding)

01:12:15.304 --> 01:12:18.240
I think this is...

01:12:18.240 --> 01:12:19.073
Oh.

01:12:23.050 --> 01:12:24.612
- You don't have to answer it.

01:12:24.612 --> 01:12:25.445
- Yeah.

01:12:25.445 --> 01:12:26.447
(laughing)

01:12:26.447 --> 01:12:28.070
- Maybe.

01:12:28.070 --> 01:12:30.026
Oh, yeah, this...

01:12:30.026 --> 01:12:33.943
(speaking in foreign language)

01:13:29.064 --> 01:13:30.664
(interpreter laughs)

01:13:30.664 --> 01:13:34.581
(speaking in foreign language)

01:14:10.784 --> 01:14:12.790
(interpreter sighs)

01:14:12.790 --> 01:14:17.790
- Well, the climate
change is a huge process.

01:14:22.610 --> 01:14:27.430
It's very vast, it has all
kinds of sub bits and pieces,

01:14:27.430 --> 01:14:31.320
and it's somewhat a gradual process,

01:14:31.320 --> 01:14:36.320
so that we don't directly feel
it or notice it day by day.

01:14:37.800 --> 01:14:40.570
By the time that we all
feel climate change,

01:14:40.570 --> 01:14:43.893
it will be far too late
to do anything about it.

01:14:44.940 --> 01:14:47.360
So there really are two
levels of denial here.

01:14:47.360 --> 01:14:49.290
There's the denial of those

01:14:49.290 --> 01:14:52.340
who are actual climate change deniers:

01:14:52.340 --> 01:14:54.290
politicians and others.

01:14:54.290 --> 01:14:57.800
And then there's the denial
that most of us live in

01:14:57.800 --> 01:15:02.180
of climate change, where because
we don't actually feel it

01:15:02.180 --> 01:15:04.603
it's not really in our
minds most of the time.

01:15:05.670 --> 01:15:09.480
And then there are little
pockets of odd reactions.

01:15:09.480 --> 01:15:11.650
For example, Tibetans...

01:15:11.650 --> 01:15:13.610
Tibet is a very cold country.

01:15:13.610 --> 01:15:15.610
It's often hard to get warm enough,

01:15:15.610 --> 01:15:20.190
and so the global warming,
some Tibetans have greeted this

01:15:20.190 --> 01:15:22.150
with enthusiasm, (Mary laughs)
thinking it doesn't get

01:15:22.150 --> 01:15:24.583
as cold as it used to,
it's much more pleasant.

01:15:26.500 --> 01:15:29.640
As for what to do about
politicians and others

01:15:29.640 --> 01:15:34.620
who are active or outspoken
climate change deniers,

01:15:34.620 --> 01:15:39.040
the best thing might
be to appeal to support

01:15:39.040 --> 01:15:41.440
from religious and spiritual leaders

01:15:41.440 --> 01:15:43.600
because they have so much influence

01:15:43.600 --> 01:15:48.010
over their respective
communities or devotees.

01:15:48.010 --> 01:15:48.843
- [Andrew] Yes.

01:15:48.843 --> 01:15:50.290
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

01:15:50.290 --> 01:15:53.940
- So Your Holiness, the last
question is one a number

01:15:53.940 --> 01:15:56.803
of our undergraduate students asked,

01:15:58.400 --> 01:16:02.450
and that is how can students
in a university like Yale

01:16:02.450 --> 01:16:06.720
organize themselves to take
fruitful and positive action

01:16:06.720 --> 01:16:07.610
for the environment?

01:16:07.610 --> 01:16:09.317
What can they do that's practical?

01:16:12.525 --> 01:16:14.477
(sighing)

01:16:14.477 --> 01:16:18.394
(speaking in foreign language)

01:17:59.840 --> 01:18:04.610
- Of course there are several
things we can do as groups

01:18:04.610 --> 01:18:09.610
or communities, the usual types
of environmental activism,

01:18:09.810 --> 01:18:12.810
demonstrations, raising
our voice and so on,

01:18:12.810 --> 01:18:17.617
but fundamentally each of us
has to start with ourselves,

01:18:20.170 --> 01:18:25.170
because the only solid basis
for the type of courage

01:18:27.480 --> 01:18:30.940
it's going to take to
actually make these changes

01:18:30.940 --> 01:18:34.980
comes when you have made
changes in your own life

01:18:34.980 --> 01:18:38.640
and have begun to feel
the effects of that,

01:18:38.640 --> 01:18:41.500
and that courage can't
be taken away from you,

01:18:41.500 --> 01:18:43.730
so you won't lose it in the process

01:18:43.730 --> 01:18:46.540
of a long career of activism.

01:18:46.540 --> 01:18:51.540
Otherwise, if we attempt to
change or convince others

01:18:52.690 --> 01:18:55.130
to change without having changed the way

01:18:55.130 --> 01:18:59.480
we live our lives ourselves,
we will be fueling ourselves

01:18:59.480 --> 01:19:03.680
not with an internal courage
but with expectations.

01:19:03.680 --> 01:19:07.080
And when others don't change
the way we would like them to,

01:19:07.080 --> 01:19:08.450
we'll become disillusioned

01:19:08.450 --> 01:19:12.060
and possibly totally
burned out altogether.

01:19:12.060 --> 01:19:16.060
So we have to start whatever
we may do, on a social

01:19:16.060 --> 01:19:17.680
or communal level, we have to start

01:19:17.680 --> 01:19:21.510
by making individual
changes in our own lives.

01:19:21.510 --> 01:19:24.970
And then another thing
that is necessary is

01:19:24.970 --> 01:19:29.970
to have a support community, a
supportive community of those

01:19:30.100 --> 01:19:35.100
who share one's own environmental
aspirations and ideals.

01:19:36.850 --> 01:19:38.050
- Thank you.

01:19:38.050 --> 01:19:38.883
- [Andrew] Thank you.

01:19:38.883 --> 01:19:39.716
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

01:19:39.716 --> 01:19:43.340
- We'd like to conclude
by thanking His Holiness

01:19:43.340 --> 01:19:47.480
for reigniting hope this evening.

01:19:47.480 --> 01:19:51.530
And I'd like to say it was 20 years ago

01:19:51.530 --> 01:19:55.690
that we began a project
at Harvard actually

01:19:55.690 --> 01:20:00.010
with one of the first conferences
on Buddhism and ecology.

01:20:00.010 --> 01:20:01.470
- Yes.
- And then the other

01:20:01.470 --> 01:20:04.630
world religions and ecology.

01:20:04.630 --> 01:20:09.630
But it is our great joy that
Yale has picked up this idea

01:20:10.000 --> 01:20:13.170
that religions and
ecology can work together,

01:20:13.170 --> 01:20:14.910
which you have highlighted today.

01:20:14.910 --> 01:20:19.910
And our school of Forestry
and Environmental Studies,

01:20:20.070 --> 01:20:23.600
with the students there and
students from Religious Studies

01:20:23.600 --> 01:20:26.940
and Divinity, I think are giving us hope

01:20:26.940 --> 01:20:28.950
because they understand this,

01:20:28.950 --> 01:20:31.400
they want to work with your communities,

01:20:31.400 --> 01:20:34.590
and we want to figure
ways into the future.

01:20:34.590 --> 01:20:37.610
I want to end though with a special thanks

01:20:37.610 --> 01:20:42.610
to our Dean, Peter Crane,
who has held up this effort

01:20:42.700 --> 01:20:43.660
between the School

01:20:43.660 --> 01:20:47.440
of Forestry Environmental
Studies and Divinity.

01:20:47.440 --> 01:20:51.680
And tomorrow night we're going
to continue the conversation

01:20:51.680 --> 01:20:55.670
with Peter Crane, scientists,
ethicists and so on

01:20:55.670 --> 01:20:59.590
between these two schools to examine

01:20:59.590 --> 01:21:04.280
the new papal encyclical
which will be coming out

01:21:04.280 --> 01:21:07.680
this summer exactly on this issue,

01:21:07.680 --> 01:21:10.690
which can affect a billion Catholics,

01:21:10.690 --> 01:21:14.270
two billion Christians, but be
in dialogue with the efforts

01:21:14.270 --> 01:21:15.460
that you're doing.

01:21:15.460 --> 01:21:20.460
So tomorrow night at Lindsay
Hall on High Street at 5:30,

01:21:21.300 --> 01:21:24.120
we invite you to join in this discussion

01:21:24.120 --> 01:21:26.710
that will complement the wonderful words

01:21:26.710 --> 01:21:28.540
that you have given us tonight.

01:21:28.540 --> 01:21:31.710
Thank you for your hope and
please come back to Yale.

01:21:31.710 --> 01:21:32.543
- Yes.
- Visit us.

01:21:32.543 --> 01:21:33.872
- Please do come back.
- Thank you.

01:21:33.872 --> 01:21:37.039
(audience applauding)

01:21:40.133 --> 01:21:41.158
- Thanks a lot.

